; ==========================================================================
; STELLAR INVADERS v02  -  now the invaders shoot back
; ==========================================================================
; Two rows of six invaders march across the screen and drop a line each time they
; reach an edge, and they drop bombs on you as they come. The gun sits on line 23.
; LEFT and RIGHT arrows move it, SPACE fires, and Q quits; a and d also work if
; your terminal sends no arrow keys. One rocket up and one bomb down at a time.
; Clear all twelve and you win; let them land, or take a bomb on the gun, and you
; lose.
;
; THE BOMBS. Every frame with no bomb in the air there is a one-in-twenty-four
; chance of one starting. The ship that drops it is picked at random from all
; twelve, and if that one has already been destroyed no bomb falls this time -
; which naturally thins the fire as you clear the formation. Bombs fall every
; OTHER frame, half the speed of your rocket, so they can be dodged.
;
; NO REGISTER WAS FREE for the bomb, so its column and line live in memory at
; $4972 and $4974, beside the arrow-key state. Note which way the accumulator
; opcodes go: COPYXA moves a REGISTER INTO the accumulator, COPYAX goes the other
; way, and COPYAMX writes the accumulator out to the memory a register points at.
;
; SHIP TABLE. The twelve ships are twelve bytes at $4960 - $01 alive, $00
; destroyed - reached with READIDX and WRITEIDX, which index a program-relative
; base by a register and leave that register alone. Ships 0-5 are the top row,
; 6-11 the bottom.
;
; ONE DIVIDE ANSWERS "WHICH SHIP". Ships are three columns wide on a six-column
; pitch, so DIVXY of (rocket column - formation column) by six gives the ship
; number as the quotient and the offset within it as the remainder: 0-2 is a hit,
; 3-5 the gap. The remainder is also what the erase needs to find the ship's
; left-hand column, so it is kept rather than recomputed - an earlier draft
; divided again by a register that by then held the ship INDEX, and for ship 0
; that is a divide by zero, which raises Error $07 and stops the VM.
;
; ARROW KEYS ARE THREE BYTES. Left is ESC [ D and right is ESC [ C, so the program
; keeps a small state machine at $4970: 0 = waiting, 1 = seen ESC, 2 = seen ESC
; and [. That is also why ESC is not the quit key here.
;
; DRAWING. The formation is redrawn only on the frames it actually marches - every
; eighth. Redrawing it every frame costs about 110 bytes of output per frame and
; makes the ships shimmer. Each row is drawn as one run starting a column left of
; the formation, with dead ships drawn as blanks, so the same pass erases the
; trailing column and the destroyed ships.
;
; REGISTERS. R1 gun column, R2 rocket column, R3 rocket line (0 = none), R4
; formation column, R5 formation top line, R6 direction, R7 frame counter, R8
; ships left, R9-R12 scratch, R13 line and R14 column for POSXY, R15 = ten. The
; end-of-game message is passed in R12, because R14 belongs to POSXY - passing it
; in R14 means the position call overwrites it, which once made all three endings
; print YOU WIN.
;
; ADDRESSES. The LOADXI values in the code are PROGRAM-RELATIVE - $0960 means
; $4960 at run time - but the data D-lines below carry the ABSOLUTE address.
; Writing the data at the relative address drops it on the firmware: $09A0 is
; inside the opcode dispatch table.
;
; HW-VERIFIED 2026-08-22 on the Elf2K - Craig flashed and ran it.
; Sim-verified with an autoplayer driving real key sequences: sitting still gets
; the gun destroyed, Q abandons the game, the formation marches down and lands,
; bombs are drawn down the full height of the screen and erase themselves, and
; ships that have been destroyed stop firing.
;
; NOTE every D-line here is 16 bytes or fewer. The Elf2K monitor's input buffer is
; 64 characters - "D aaaa " plus 19 bytes - and it silently keeps what fits.
;
; 342 STELLAR commands, $4000-$43BB (956 bytes of code).
; Data at $4900, 94 bytes, past the end marker.
; Data at $49A0, 30 bytes, past the end marker.
; Data at $49C0, 25 bytes, past the end marker.
; Data at $49E0, 15 bytes, past the end marker.
; Data at $4A00, 28 bytes, past the end marker.
;
; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
; DISASSEMBLY  -  commented out; the loader skips it
; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
; D 4000 CD             START                  Start of program, and run the label pre-pass
; D 4001 11 0F 00 0A    LOADXI R15 000A        Ten, for splitting numbers into digits
; D 4005 11 0E 09 00    LOADXI R14 0900        Point at the title text
; D 4009 C1 0E          PRINTSTR R14           Print it
; D 400B C3             RAND                   Stir the generator while we wait
; D 400C CC             SERST                  A key yet?
; D 400D A1 00 0B       IFFALSEI SEED
; D 4010 CB             SERIN                  Take it and discard it
; D 4011 11 01 00 26    LOADXI R1 0026         Gun starts at column 38
; D 4015 11 02 00 00    LOADXI R2 0000         No rocket in flight
; D 4019 11 03 00 00    LOADXI R3 0000
; D 401D 11 04 00 08    LOADXI R4 0008         The formation starts at column 8
; D 4021 11 05 00 02    LOADXI R5 0002         top row on line 2
; D 4025 11 06 00 00    LOADXI R6 0000         marching right
; D 4029 11 07 00 00    LOADXI R7 0000         Frame counter
; D 402D 11 08 00 0C    LOADXI R8 000C         Twelve ships to shoot down
; D 4031 11 0B 09 70    LOADXI R11 0970        Clear the arrow-key state
; D 4035 32 0B 00 00    COPYMX2I R11 0000
; D 4039 11 0B 09 74    LOADXI R11 0974        and make sure no bomb is falling
; D 403D 32 0B 00 00    COPYMX2I R11 0000
; D 4041 11 09 00 00    LOADXI R9 0000         Ship index
; D 4045 10 00 01       LOADAI 0001            Alive
; D 4048 2F 09 09 60    WRITEIDX R9 0960
; D 404C 42 09          INCX R9
; D 404E 86 09 00 0C    TESTXI R9 000C         All twelve done?
; D 4052 A1 00 45       IFFALSEI MKALIVE
; D 4055 CA 1B          SEROUTI 1B             ''
; D 4057 CA 5B          SEROUTI 5B             '['
; D 4059 CA 32          SEROUTI 32             '2'
; D 405B CA 4A          SEROUTI 4A             'J'
; D 405D CA 1B          SEROUTI 1B             ''
; D 405F CA 5B          SEROUTI 5B             '['
; D 4061 CA 3F          SEROUTI 3F             '?'
; D 4063 CA 32          SEROUTI 32             '2'
; D 4065 CA 35          SEROUTI 35             '5'
; D 4067 CA 6C          SEROUTI 6C             'l'
; D 4069 BD 02 D6       CALL DRAWALL           Both rows of ships
; D 406C BD 02 8F       CALL DRAWGUN           and the gun
; D 406F 42 07          INCX R7                Count the frame
; D 4071 CC             SERST                  Anything typed?
; D 4072 A1 01 15       IFFALSEI NOKEY         No
; D 4075 CB             SERIN                  Read the byte
; D 4076 21 0C          COPYAX 0C              Keep it in R12
; D 4078 11 0B 09 70    LOADXI R11 0970        Where are we in an escape sequence?
; D 407C 33 0B          COPYMXA 0B
; D 407E 81 00 01       TESTAI 0001            Waiting for the '[' ?
; D 4081 A0 00 C0       IFTRUEI KST1
; D 4084 33 0B          COPYMXA 0B
; D 4086 81 00 02       TESTAI 0002            Waiting for the letter?
; D 4089 A0 00 D2       IFTRUEI KST2
; D 408C 86 0C 00 1B    TESTXI R12 001B        ESC starts an arrow key
; D 4090 A1 00 9A       IFFALSEI KNOTESC
; D 4093 32 0B 00 01    COPYMX2I R11 0001      Remember we have seen ESC
; D 4097 B0 01 15       GAL NOKEY
; D 409A 86 0C 00 20    TESTXI R12 0020        Space fires
; D 409E A0 01 05       IFTRUEI FIRE
; D 40A1 86 0C 00 71    TESTXI R12 0071        q quits
; D 40A5 A0 02 64       IFTRUEI QUIT
; D 40A8 86 0C 00 51    TESTXI R12 0051        and so does Q
; D 40AC A0 02 64       IFTRUEI QUIT
; D 40AF 86 0C 00 61    TESTXI R12 0061        a moves left, for terminals that send no arrows
; D 40B3 A0 00 E7       IFTRUEI GOLEFT
; D 40B6 86 0C 00 64    TESTXI R12 0064        d moves right
; D 40BA A0 00 F6       IFTRUEI GORIGHT
; D 40BD B0 01 15       GAL NOKEY
; D 40C0 32 0B 00 00    COPYMX2I R11 0000      Whatever it was, the sequence restarts
; D 40C4 86 0C 00 5B    TESTXI R12 005B        Was it '[' ?
; D 40C8 A1 01 15       IFFALSEI NOKEY
; D 40CB 32 0B 00 02    COPYMX2I R11 0002      Now expect the letter
; D 40CF B0 01 15       GAL NOKEY
; D 40D2 32 0B 00 00    COPYMX2I R11 0000      Sequence finished
; D 40D6 86 0C 00 44    TESTXI R12 0044        'D' is the left arrow
; D 40DA A0 00 E7       IFTRUEI GOLEFT
; D 40DD 86 0C 00 43    TESTXI R12 0043        'C' is the right arrow
; D 40E1 A0 00 F6       IFTRUEI GORIGHT
; D 40E4 B0 01 15       GAL NOKEY
; D 40E7 86 01 00 02    TESTXI R1 0002         Already hard left?
; D 40EB A0 01 15       IFTRUEI NOKEY
; D 40EE 43 01          DECX R1
; D 40F0 BD 02 8F       CALL DRAWGUN
; D 40F3 B0 01 15       GAL NOKEY
; D 40F6 86 01 00 4D    TESTXI R1 004D         Already hard right? (column 77)
; D 40FA A0 01 15       IFTRUEI NOKEY
; D 40FD 42 01          INCX R1
; D 40FF BD 02 8F       CALL DRAWGUN
; D 4102 B0 01 15       GAL NOKEY
; D 4105 8C 03          TESTXZ R3              Is a rocket already in flight?
; D 4107 A1 01 15       IFFALSEI NOKEY         Yes - only one at a time
; D 410A 23 00 12       COPYXY R1 R2           It leaves from the gun
; D 410D 52 02 00 01    ADDXI R2 0001          from the middle of the barrel
; D 4111 11 03 00 16    LOADXI R3 0016         starting one line above the gun
; D 4115 8C 03          TESTXZ R3              Anything in flight?
; D 4117 A0 01 3F       IFTRUEI NOSHOT         No
; D 411A 23 00 3D       COPYXY R3 R13          Erase it from where it was
